{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update-stage29.json\", \"summary\": \"Final source item for the run's remaining budget: the shutdown contract, recorded as a behaviour plus an explained investigation with no defect claim (valid:true).\\n\\nWhat the captured code promises: `Shutdown` is idempotent and two-phased — the first call takes the shutdown lock, closes the shutdown channel, records the node as shut down, moves it to the Shutdown state and returns a shutdown future bound to the node, while later calls return a future whose node pointer is deliberately nil so nothing is closed twice (api.go:1012-1025). Because the state change happens under the lock before the future is returned, a caller that ignores the future still observes the shutdown; a caller that waits gets the stronger guarantee, since the bound future's `Error` waits on the node's routine group and then closes the transport when it implements `WithClose` (future.go:167-180). In-flight work is not orphaned: the `processLogs` batch handoff selects on the shutdown channel and answers `ErrRaftShutdown` for a batch it could not dispatch (raft.go:1300-1310), and the run loops return out of their selects so their own pending futures are answered by the cleanup paths already recorded in this run (for example the leader's leadership-lost responses).\\n\\nThe boundary worth naming, and the only one: a caller who never waits on the returned future has requested shutdown rather than confirmed it — the return value is what waits for background work. Limitations state that this is source-only, that no shutdown was raced against in-flight operations, that only the `processLogs` handoff was cited as a response path while other shutdown sites were not enumerated, and that transports without `WithClose` are awaited but not closed (read, not exercised).\"}"}}
